{
  "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
  "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Harold I. Goodman",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOODMAN: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This c case brings up for review a fairly straightforward but nonetheless essential question as it applies to Federal practice and procedure, namely, whether or not notice pleading is sufficient with compliance with Rule 8(a)(2), or whether or not the rule requires some element of fact pleading to overcome a Rule 12(b)(6) motion to dismiss."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: You don't deny that some facts have to be stated, do you?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: You would have to also allege, wouldn't you, that you were discriminated against because of your race, or because of your nationality?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jeffrey P. Minear",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MINEAR: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The court of appeals in this case clearly erred in ruling that the pleadings, in this case the complaint, were insufficient. The Court's -- this Court's ruling in Conley v. Gibson makes clear that notice is what's essential in the complaint. In this case, the complaint set forth a short and plain statement of the claim, and it indicated a request for relief."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Suppose a person simply feels -- he's been fired, and he thinks his work was good, and the employer said it was bad, so he thinks, they couldn't have fired me because my work was bad. It's good. What reason could there have been? Well, I sense an anti-Hungarian atmosphere in this office. That's it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: How could a judge refuse discovery on that -- on these --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: So then, what the Second Circuit is actually saying is, since the judge can't refuse discovery in my case, let's go back and look and see what the cause of action is, and the cause of action is such that my case doesn't really fall within it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: May I ask you one question? In paragraph 31 of the complaint, they refer to this memorandum as outlining the plaintiff's grievances and requesting -- outlining grievances, then the memorandum was put into the record by the defendant and the judge reviewed the memorandum and thought it didn't really show any discrimination."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: You'd have to allow discovery anyway before you could rule under 12(b)(6), right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: That's exactly what I meant, that you couldn't get rid of the case on the basis of summary judgment without allowing discovery, so there's basically no way to prevent being subjected to discovery on the basis of a claim by somebody who just suspects, with no reason to suspect, that he has been fired because he's Hungarian."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What is the standard of confidence, the standard of belief that the plaintiff and the attorney must have before starting the action?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, doesn't he have to investigate the state of mind of the plaintiff to determine is bona fides, or does he -- is there some objective standard implicit in the good faith, there have to be some objective basis for the good faith belief?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: So we can look at the case without looking at the memorandum?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Yes, but here he alleged that he was terminated because of his nationality and because of his age."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0012",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: I thought there was a statement here that other people who had been -- were not let go, people for whom there was cause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0001-0013",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Sorry, then I'm confused, because I -- you don't -- an inference isn't the kind of thing that you allege."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What was lacking in Conley? What was lacking?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. BRODY: There was nothing lacking in the complaint in Conley. In fact, if you look at it, it alleges all the elements of the claim, and it does so on a rather specific basis."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But McDonnell Douglas I don't think was ever meant to be a pleading requirement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-00-1853",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "14e4587efc9740e598e182770b2c451cf79c0c900d381db6c2fb57b3cdf83441",
      "evidence_id": "ev-14e4587efc97-0002-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. BRODY: That was a dismissal on a 12(b)(6)."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
